```rust
/// Renames a stored note: changes its id to `new_id` and its title to
/// `new_title`, leaving body, tags, and pinned state unchanged. The old
/// file is removed so `load_all` sees exactly one note with the new id.
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            format!("malformed note file: {}", old_path.display()),
        ));
    }
    if parts[0] != old_id {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            format!("note id mismatch in {}: expected {}", old_path.display(), old_id),
        ));
    }
    let pinned_flag = parts[2];
    let new_content = format!(
        "{}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
        new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned_flag, SEP, parts[3]
    );
    let new_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id));
    fs::write(&new_path, new_content)?;
    if new_id != old_id {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Wait — one more edge case: what if a file with new_id already exists and is a different note? We overwrite it. That's standard rename semantics, and load_all must return exactly one note. No problem.

Order: write the new file first, then delete the old file. If the delete fails, load_all will return a duplicate — but that's a failure case where we return an Err anyway, and the caller knows something went wrong. Data isn't lost.

Another minor point: `parts[2]` — pinned flag. What if the saved file was written by an old version and pinned is some other string? save() always writes "0"/"1". load_all compares against "1". Preserving the raw string round-trips exactly. Good.

Also, the `parts.len() != 4` check: if the body contains SEP, splitn(4) returns exactly 4 parts with the remainder in parts[3]. Good — that's why splitn is used.

Note: what if the title contains SEP? splitn(4) splits the first 3 occurrences, so a title containing "\n---\n" would be broken — but that's an existing limitation of the format, not something I should fix.

